five weeks ago general abdul sata  mursakwal stepped into the role of  afghanistan's interior minister in all  he commands a force of 130 000 people  today he is driving to wardak a province  west of kabul much of the province is  controlled by the taliban for government  personnel traveling by road is rare and  requires an escort of special forces  in the last three months the taliban has  more than doubled its territory and in  the last week started to take provincial  capitals  what's going through your head at the  moment about those provinces how how  worried are you  and and how do you stop more falling  we are working in three phases the first  is to stop the defeats the second is to  re-gather our forces to create security  rings around the cities  all those soldiers that abandoned their  post were bringing them back to their  posts the third is to begin offensive  operations  these trips are about boosting morale he  admits that has suffered with the  advances the taliban has made after the  taliban attempted to assassinate the  defense minister and promise to target  other government leaders security around  minister mirzaquel has never been  tighter a simple task of visiting  checkpoints requires this many special  forces  he has survived multiple taliban  assassination attempts in his previous  jobs as the governor of kunar and kunduz  i'm a soldier a soldier never gets  frightened  when we wear this uniform it means we  are ready for death i'm not worried  about it  he says a lot of the government's losses  are as a result of them losing control  of roads and highways many areas must be  resupplied by air and after the u.s  withdrew they lost a lot of that  capability  unfortunately with their withdrawal the  fighting started in 400 areas of the  country  we have very limited air support the  helicopters have been busy with moving  supplies and evacuating our dead and  injured forces  so they are delegating to local leaders  empowering them to recruit within their  community to rise against the taliban  there are concerns from the  international community about these  uprising forces at the moment  but all their members will eventually  merge into the afghan national security  forces  wardak's governor says he already has  300 men fighting in one force but he  complained he could only provide weapons  for two-thirds the rest he said he  cannot provide with water let alone  money or arms  for the last few months people are  waiting for their weapons but  unfortunately the promises i made to  these people were not fulfilled but  there is no doubt in their loyalty and  desire to help  outside the room the minister's men wait  wardak police and soldiers join them  this talk of last night's gains and  losses where they may be sent next and  how their many injuries are healing some  briefly leave to push off a taliban  assault  [Music]  thousands of police officers have  abandoned their posts some recent months  the interior ministry says they are  returning and will be retrained then  sent back to the provinces  they say 5 000 people have signed up for  the police force in just the last three  weeks with another two thousand  graduating this weekend  the government strategy is slowly  becoming clear but its implementation is  still fraught and the stakes have never  been higher  if the taliban are watching what would  you say to them  i'm asking the taliban to stop their  brutality leave killing sit down with  love and we should find solutions i'm  asking them not to destroy buildings or  our achievements  let's come and sit together and make a  coalition government one that would be  acceptable to all sides the sooner we do  this the better  yet they need leverage on the  battlefield to force the group to make  political compromises and every day with  every loss that leverage weakens  charlotte ballas al-jazeera wardak  afghanistan
 

